-- Record each write session's staging directory on its `writes` rows
-- (issue #25, playbook T8 remainder).
--
-- An interrupted `volume write` leaves the tape unsealed and its `writes`/
-- `write_positions` rows in the `interrupted` state. Resuming it requires the
-- session's `Layout` — and the Layout CANNOT be regenerated by calling
-- `build()` again after a process restart:
--
--   * `BuildInputs::created_at` is `chrono::Utc::now()` at `volume_write`
--     call time and is persisted nowhere, so a second build produces
--     different ID-thunk bytes and therefore a different sha256 for File 0.
--   * `BuildInputs::mam_loads` is `read_mam`'s `load_count`, which increments
--     on every cartridge load, so any resume that involved reloading the tape
--     sees a different value — again, different ID-thunk bytes.
--
-- `SealedPending::confirm` diffs the front index read back off the medium
-- against the Layout it holds, so a regenerated Layout would report a
-- mismatch and QUARANTINE a perfectly good tape. `docs/design/layout-session.md`'s
-- Resume rule is explicit that the frozen generated zones "re-hash
-- byte-identical" — re-hash the frozen files, not re-generate them — and
-- `layout_model.rs`'s `ContentSource` doc comment records the same
-- conclusion.
--
-- The frozen files live under the session's staging directory
-- (`v2-open-questions.md` §2.2, "materialize-to-staging"), which until now
-- was known only to the in-memory `BuiltLayout`. This column persists that
-- pointer so a restarted process can find it. `ValidatedLayout::plan` is the
-- only writer: plan is never called on resume (`writes` has
-- `UNIQUE(stage_set_id, volume_id)`; resume reuses the existing rows), so it
-- is also the only place the value could be written.
--
-- Nullable: every pre-existing row predates the concept and has no session
-- directory. Such a row is not resumable — only abortable — and
-- `InterruptedSession::rehydrate` says so explicitly rather than guessing a
-- path.
ALTER TABLE writes ADD COLUMN session_dir TEXT;
